.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 118 PM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

An upper level trough dominates much of the West Coast`s weather
this afternoon as the closed low sits over the PacNW and broad
troughing extends southward through California. To it`s west, over
the eastern Pacific, high pressure is being observed. Satellite
imagery shows these too features quite well, with visible satellite
showing the base of the trough over Central and Southern California.
Most of our skies this afternoon remain clear; however, if you`re in
the interior Central Coast region, you may see some small puffs of
cumulus clouds building over the ridges. So far there hasn`t been
any measurable rain reported or observed, likely due to the dry air
in place and limited cloud growth.

Tonight, look for skies to clear and for another chilly fall night.
Temperatures should generally similar to or a few degrees higher
than last night. Tuesday`s forecast will be similar to today, though
skies across the region should be mostly clear with high
temperatures being a few degrees warmer than today.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 118 PM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026
(Wednesday through next Sunday)

An upper level high pressure system over the eastern Pacific will
continue to build and expand eastward towards the West Coast. This
will help push the upper level trough out of the region late Tuesday
into Wednesday. During this time frame we will see a period of
offshore flow during the overnight hours. As the high continues to
build and the low moves east, each night will bring a renewed chance
of these offshore winds. Especially late in the week, as the upper
level trough elongates and then splits. When this occurs, the trough
situates over the Desert Southwest and around southern California.
Ridging remains over the northern half of the state which should lead
to an increase of offshore flow and our warming trend. Ensembles and
models then show signals of quasi-zonal flow and weak ridging for
the weekend. Temperatures should decrease in response to the pattern
change. For fire weather concerns, please see the fire weather
discussion below.

