.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 118 PM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026

There will be a few bursts of offshore winds this week, generally
during the overnight hours. The first burst looks to occur tonight,
particularly highlight the interior North Bay Mountains, where we
could see gusts range from 20-35 mph, perhaps even up to 40 mph for
the exposed ridges and the higher elevations. After that, the next
area of concern we are monitoring is Wednesday night into Thursday
morning, where the pressure gradient should be a bit more tight due
to upper level wind patterns. Majority of the area should see
offshore flow, in the north to northeast component , with gusts
closer to 25-35 mph, locally higher perhaps up to 45 mph for exposed
ridges, gaps, and passes. Warm and dry weather will be on tap as
well, which will lead to an elevated threat for the higher terrain
areas. Another round of offshore winds is expected Thursday night
into Friday, but appears to be weaker than the previous two.

&&

.BEACHES...
Issued at 1031 AM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026

Compare to the last few days conditions along the coast are
gradually improving as longer period swell trains are decreasing.
However, buoy reports Monday morning continue to advertise
moderate to longer period swells (albeit smaller heights)
entering the coastal waters. As such, elevated surf zone
conditions lingering. Additionally, moderate period west-northwest
swell will arrive Monday afternoon lingering into Monday evening.
As such, Beach Hazard Statement remains in effect through this
evening. The large tidal swings will also result in coastal
erosion as water and waves are brought farther up the beach than
normal, especially during high tide. Remain off jetties, piers,
rocks, and other waterside infrastructure as waves can sweep
across the shoreline without warning, pulling people into the sea.
Never turn your back on the ocean!

